AlignmentsColor.text:
# AlignmentsColor (Display setting config, alignments plugin)

The alignments displays' `encoding.color`: one color for every read in `value`, or a field of each read. A preset field, listed below, colors by its levels or bins, `tags.XX` reads a SAM tag, and any other name reads the read as a facet over it does: `mateAssembly`, a feature attribute, a dotted path into one or a `jexl:` expression. A string is the field. Unlike a feature track, which deals colors in the order values appear, a tag or attribute with no `scale.domain`, `range` or `unknown` colors each value by its text alone, so the value keeps its color in every region and session. `split`,…

```js
{ type: 'LinearAlignmentsDisplay', encoding: { color: 'strand' } }
```
```js
{
  type: 'LinearAlignmentsDisplay',
  encoding: {
    color: {
      field: 'tags.HP',
      scale: {
        domain: ['1', '2'],
        range: ['#d95f02', '#1b9e77'],
        names: ['Maternal', 'Paternal'],
        invalid: '#cccccc',
      },
    },
  },
}
```
```js
{
  type: 'LinearAlignmentsDisplay',
  encoding: {
    color: { field: 'tags.NM', scale: { type: 'linear', scheme: 'viridis' } },
  },
}
```
```js
{
  type: 'LinearAlignmentsDisplay',
  encoding: { color: { field: 'insertSize', scale: { domain: [150, 600] } } },
}
```
```js
{
  type: 'LinearAlignmentsDisplay',
  encoding: {
    color: {
      field: 'pairOrientation',
      scale: { domain: ['RR'], range: ['#d95f02'] },
    },
  },
}
```

## Slots
- `value: maybeColor`: The fill of every read while no field paints. Arcs and pair orientations keep the theme's colors. Unset, the theme's read color.
- `scale: AlignmentsColorScale sub-schema`: How the field's values map to colors, Vega-Lite's scale object.
- `scale.invalid: maybeColor`: The fill of a read with no value under the field, Vega-Lite's word for a missing value's output: no tag, no attribute, or no mate under `mateRefName`. A `''` in `domain` colors it by its `range` entry instead.
- `scale.unknown: maybeColor`: The fill of a read whose tag, attribute, mate reference or MAPQ an ordinal `domain` leaves out, d3's `ordinal.unknown`: `unknown: "#999"` beside `domain: ["1", "2"]` on `tags.HP` paints every other haplotype grey, and the key lists them as…
- `scale.names: stringArray = []`: Names for each level or value `range` colors, in the same order, in the key, the hovers and the arc key: `names: ["Maternal", "Paternal"]` beside `domain: ["1", "2"]` on `tags.HP`.
- `legend: AlignmentsColorLegend sub-schema`: The key this channel draws.
- `field: string = ''`: field to color each read by: a preset field by its levels or bins; tags.XX reads a SAM tag; any other name reads as a facet over it does: mateAssembly, a feature attribute, a dotted path or a jexl: expression; split, spliced, pairing and…
- `scale.type: maybeStringEnum (ordinal, linear, threshold)`: ordinal a range color per value; linear a ramp over mapq or a numeric tag or attribute between domainMin and domainMax; threshold the bins domain cuts; unset, threshold over insertSize, insertSizeAndOrientation and mapq, and ordinal over…
- `scale.domain: stringArray = []`: for an ordinal scale, the values that take the range first, in order: a preset field's levels, insertSize's short, normal and long, with unmappedMate and interchrom under both insert-size fields, '' a read with no value, a MAPQ, 255 for…
- `scale.range: colorArray = []`: CSS colors an ordinal scale hands its domain in order, or with no domain a preset field's levels in their preset order, a threshold scale its bins, or a linear scale's stops, evenly spaced; a level left out keeps its default; empty uses…
- `scale.scheme: maybeStringEnum (viridis, magma, inferno, cividis, juicebox, fall, reds, blues, redblue, purpleorange, redgreyblue, redyellowblue)`: a named ramp: a ramp scale runs along it, a threshold that writes cuts takes one color per interval from end to end, and an ordinal scale that lists a domain one per value; range's colors, where it lists any, win over it
- `scale.reverse: maybeBoolean`: turns the ramp round, so its last color paints the bottom of a ramp's domain or a threshold's lowest interval; unset follows the field's preset, else runs as written
- `scale.domainMid: maybeNumber`: the value the ramp's middle stop sits at, so a diverging ramp centres somewhere other than the middle of the domain, both sides on one scale: the farther end of the domain reaches its end color and equal distances from the middle take…
- `scale.domainMin: maybeNumber`: the bottom of a ramp's domain; unset follows the loaded values
- `scale.domainMax: maybeNumber`: the top of a ramp's domain; unset follows the loaded values
- `scale.domainQuantile: number = 1`: the quantile an open end of a ramp follows over the loaded values: 1 their extremes, 0.99 clips the outermost 1% at each end, each sign measured on its own — advanced
- `legend.title: maybeString`: key title; unset keeps the display's own heading, "" draws none
- `legend.descending: maybeBoolean`: threshold key lists the highest interval first; unset follows the field's preset, else the lowest first

CytobandAdapter.text:
# CytobandAdapter (Adapter config, core)

Reads an assembly's cytoband file, which the linear genome view draws as ideogram banding in its overview bar.

Slots go inside a track's adapter: "adapter": { "type": "CytobandAdapter", ... }.

Shorthand: uri, baseUri may replace the location slot.

Goes on an ASSEMBLY, under `cytobands` — not on a track. It draws the
ideogram banding in the linear genome view's overview bar:
```js
{
  name: 'hg38',
  sequence: {
    type: 'ReferenceSequenceTrack',
    trackId: 'hg38-ReferenceSequenceTrack',
    adapter: {
      type: 'BgzipFastaAdapter',
      uri: 'https://example.com/hg38.fa.gz',
    },
  },
  cytobands: {
    adapter: {
      type: 'CytobandAdapter',
      uri: 'https://example.com/hg38.cytoBand.txt.gz',
    },
  },
}
```

## Slots
- `cytobandLocation: maybeFileLocation`: location of a UCSC-style `cytoBand.txt` (`chrom start end name gieStain`), which draws the ideogram banding in the view's overview bar. May be gzipped. Configured on an assembly, not on a track.

FeatureColor.text:
# FeatureColor (Display setting config, core)

The canvas feature displays' `encoding.color`: a CSS color or `jexl:` callback in `value`, or a field whose values each take a range color, whose numbers each take the color of the interval between cut points they fall in, or whose numbers run along a color ramp, with a key. A string is the field; a written field always paints. `scale.type: "identity"` keeps each feature's color, whether a file's itemRgb or what a `value` callback returns, and names the colors `scale.domain` lists in the key. Like UCSC's `itemRgb on`, it paints an itemRgb of `0,0,0` or `0` black, which otherwise reads as no…

```js
{ type: 'LinearBasicDisplay', encoding: { color: { value: 'goldenrod' } } }
```
```js
{
  type: 'LinearBasicDisplay',
  encoding: {
    color: { field: 'gene_biotype', scale: { range: ['#1f77b4', '#ff7f0e'] } },
  },
}
```
```js
{
  type: 'LinearBasicDisplay',
  encoding: {
    color: {
      field: 'type',
      scale: {
        domain: ['prophage', 'plasmid'],
        range: ['#d95f02', '#1b9e77'],
        unknown: '#cccccc',
      },
    },
  },
}
```
```js
{
  type: 'LinearBasicDisplay',
  encoding: {
    color: {
      field: 'score',
      scale: {
        type: 'threshold',
        domain: [0.5, 0.9],
        range: ['#c6dbef', '#6baed6', '#08519c'],
      },
    },
  },
}
```
```js
{
  type: 'LinearBasicDisplay',
  encoding: { color: { field: 'score', scale: { scheme: 'viridis', domainMin: 0 } } },
}
```
```js
{
  type: 'LinearMultiRowFeatureDisplay',
  encoding: {
    color: {
      scale: {
        type: 'identity',
        domain: ['rgb(255,0,0)', 'rgb(0,128,0)'],
        names: ['Active TSS', 'Strong transcription'],
      },
    },
  },
}
```

## Slots
- `value: maybeColor`: A CSS color, or a jexl callback over `feature` returning one. Unset, a feature's own BED itemRgb paints it if it has one, else goldenrod. Alignments' `value` is constant-only; here a callback stays, since the worker evaluates it per box,……
- `scale: FeatureColorScale sub-schema`: How the field's values map to colors, Vega-Lite's scale object.
- `legend: FeatureColorLegend sub-schema`: The key this channel draws.
- `field: featureField = ''`: a feature field, or a jexl expression over feature, whose values each take one range color, with a key; a transcript and its parts paint the transcript's value, or its gene's where the transcript has none; strand paints forward tomato and…
- `scale.type: maybeStringEnum`: ordinal a range color per value of field; threshold a range color per interval between the cut points in domain; linear or log a color along a ramp from domainMin to domainMax; identity paints each feature's own color, as a channel with no…
- `scale.domain: stringArray = []`: the values that take the range first, in order; a value left out takes a color no listed value or value met before it paints, the first time the track shows it, and keeps it. Under threshold, the ascending cut points, a value on a cut…
- `scale.range: colorArray = []`: CSS colors the domain takes, in order, continuing into the default palette past its end; under threshold one per interval, one more than the cuts, an interval left out sampling the default ramp; under linear or log the ramp's stops,…
- `scale.unknown: maybeColor`: color for a value missing from domain. Unset: the next palette color where the display deals one, and the levels a preset field names, such as strand's, keep their own colors. A color: that color. "": no color dealt, so a feature paints…
- `scale.invalid: maybeColor`: color for a feature with no value under an ordinal scale's field; unset, the display's no-value color
- `scale.names: stringArray = []`: key name for each domain value, one each in order, or under threshold for each interval from the lowest; a value past the end of the list shows its own
- `scale.scheme: maybeStringEnum (viridis, magma, inferno, cividis, juicebox, fall, reds, blues, redblue, purpleorange, redgreyblue, redyellowblue)`: a named ramp: a ramp scale runs along it, a threshold that writes cuts takes one color per interval from end to end, and an ordinal scale that lists a domain one per value; range's colors, where it lists any, win over it
- `scale.reverse: maybeBoolean`: turns the ramp round, so its last color paints the bottom of a ramp's domain or a threshold's lowest interval; unset follows the field's preset, else runs as written
- `scale.domainMid: maybeNumber`: the value the ramp's middle stop sits at, so a diverging ramp centres somewhere other than the middle of the domain, both sides on one scale: the farther end of the domain reaches its end color and equal distances from the middle take…
- `scale.domainMin: maybeNumber`: the bottom of a ramp's domain; unset follows the loaded values
- `scale.domainMax: maybeNumber`: the top of a ramp's domain; unset follows the loaded values
- `scale.domainQuantile: number = 1`: the quantile an open end of a ramp follows over the loaded values: 1 their extremes, 0.99 clips the outermost 1% at each end, each sign measured on its own — advanced
- `legend.title: maybeString`: key title; unset keeps the display's own heading, "" draws none

MarkColor.text:
# MarkColor (Display setting config, marks plugin)

A mark's `encoding.color`: one CSS color or `jexl:` callback for every instance in `value`, or a `field` through `scale`, Vega-Lite's scale object: an ordinal scale (a `range` color per value), a `linear`, `log` or `symlog` scale (a ramp between `domainMin` and `domainMax`, `domainMid` placing its middle stop where a diverging ramp turns; `symlog` runs logarithmic away from 0 and linear through it, so a signed fold change ramps on both sides) or a `threshold` scale (a `range` color per interval between the cut points `domain` lists). An `identity` scale paints each feature the CSS color its…

```js
{ mark: 'bar', encoding: { y: 'score', color: { value: 'steelblue' } } }
```
```js
{ mark: 'point', encoding: { y: 'score', color: 'type' } }
```
```js
{
  mark: 'point',
  encoding: {
    y: 'score',
    color: { field: 'strand', scale: { domain: ['1', '-1'] } },
  },
}
```
```js
{
  mark: 'rect',
  encoding: {
    color: {
      field: 'score',
      scale: {
        type: 'log',
        domainMin: 1,
        domainMax: 1000,
        range: ['white', 'red'],
      },
    },
  },
}
```
```js
{
  mark: 'bar',
  encoding: {
    y: 'score',
    color: {
      field: 'score',
      scale: {
        type: 'linear',
        domainMin: -2,
        domainMax: 6,
        domainMid: 0,
        range: ['blue', 'white', 'red'],
      },
    },
  },
}
```
```js
{
  mark: 'point',
  encoding: {
    y: 'score',
    color: {
      field: 'pip',
      scale: {
        type: 'threshold',
        domain: [0.1, 0.5],
        range: ['#357ebd', '#eea236', '#d43f3a'],
      },
      legend: { title: 'PIP', descending: true },
    },
  },
}
```
```js
{
  mark: 'rect',
  encoding: {
    row: 'cellType',
    color: {
      scale: {
        type: 'identity',
        domain: ['rgb(255,0,0)', 'rgb(0,128,0)'],
        names: ['Active TSS', 'Strong transcription'],
      },
    },
  },
}
```

## Slots
- `value: maybeColor`: A CSS color, or a jexl callback over `feature` returning one, for a mark whose color is not a scale. Unset, a mark paints in the default blue, `#0068d1`, and a text mark prints in the page's text color. — callback args: feature
- `scale: MarkColorScale sub-schema`: How the field's values map to colors, Vega-Lite's scale object.
- `legend: MarkColorLegend sub-schema`: The key this channel's scale draws.
- `legend.values: stringArray = []`: The values an ordinal key lists, in this order; empty lists every value the loaded regions met. A value left out still paints, as Vega-Lite's `legend.values` and ggplot2's `breaks` leave it.
- `legend.missingLabel: maybeString`: The key's name for a feature with nothing in `field`; unset is "(no value)". Vega-Lite's legend has no such member, so it keeps its JBrowse name.
- `field: featureField = ''`: the feature field a scale reads, or a jexl expression over feature, which is slower per feature and so the opt-in
- `scale.type: maybeStringEnum (ordinal, linear, log, symlog, threshold, identity)`: ordinal hands out range colors per distinct value; linear, log and symlog read the value between domainMin and domainMax into a ramp; threshold cuts the value at the domain and hands each interval a range color; identity paints each…
- `scale.domain: stringArray = []`: for an ordinal scale, the values in legend order, walking the range from the first entry and continuing into the default palette past its end (a value left out takes a color no listed value or value met before it paints, the first time the…
- `scale.domainMin: maybeNumber`: the bottom of a ramp's domain; unset follows the loaded values
- `scale.domainMax: maybeNumber`: the top of a ramp's domain; unset follows the loaded values
- `scale.domainQuantile: number = 1`: the quantile an open end of a ramp follows over the loaded values: 1 their extremes, 0.99 clips the outermost 1% at each end, each sign measured on its own — advanced
- `scale.range: colorArray = []`: CSS colors an ordinal scale hands its domain in order, a threshold scale its intervals, or a ramp's evenly spaced stops; empty is the default palette for ordinal, the scheme, or for a threshold the default ramp sampled per interval
- `scale.unknown: maybeColor`: color for a value missing from domain. Unset: the next palette color where the display deals one, and the levels a preset field names, such as strand's, keep their own colors. A color: that color. "": no color dealt, so a feature paints…
- `scale.invalid: maybeColor`: color for a feature with no value under an ordinal scale's field; unset, the display's no-value color
- `scale.names: stringArray = []`: key name for each domain value, one each in order, or under threshold for each interval from the lowest; a value past the end of the list shows its own
- `scale.scheme: maybeStringEnum (viridis, magma, inferno, cividis, juicebox, fall, reds, blues, redblue, purpleorange, redgreyblue, redyellowblue)`: a named ramp: a ramp scale runs along it, a threshold that writes cuts takes one color per interval from end to end, and an ordinal scale that lists a domain one per value; range's colors, where it lists any, win over it
- `scale.reverse: maybeBoolean`: turns the ramp round, so its last color paints the bottom of a ramp's domain or a threshold's lowest interval; unset follows the field's preset, else runs as written
- `scale.domainMid: maybeNumber`: the value the ramp's middle stop sits at, so a diverging ramp centres somewhere other than the middle of the domain, both sides on one scale: the farther end of the domain reaches its end color and equal distances from the middle take…
- `legend.title: maybeString`: key title; unset keeps the display's own heading, "" draws none
- `legend.descending: maybeBoolean`: threshold key lists the highest interval first; unset follows the field's preset, else the lowest first

NcbiSequenceReportAliasAdapter.text:
# NcbiSequenceReportAliasAdapter (Adapter config, config plugin)

Reads refName aliases for an assembly from an NCBI `sequence_report.tsv` file.

Slots go inside a track's adapter: "adapter": { "type": "NcbiSequenceReportAliasAdapter", ... }.

Shorthand: uri, baseUri may replace the location slot.

Goes on an ASSEMBLY, under `refNameAliases` — not on a track. The file ships
beside any RefSeq assembly on NCBI datasets and aliases the RefSeq, GenBank
and UCSC-style names of every sequence at once, so it replaces a
hand-maintained chromAlias table:
```js
{
  name: 'GCF_000001405.40',
  sequence: {
    type: 'ReferenceSequenceTrack',
    trackId: 'GCF_000001405.40-ReferenceSequenceTrack',
    adapter: {
      type: 'BgzipFastaAdapter',
      uri: 'https://example.com/GCF_000001405.40.fa.gz',
    },
  },
  refNameAliases: {
    adapter: {
      type: 'NcbiSequenceReportAliasAdapter',
      uri: 'https://example.com/GCF_000001405.40_sequence_report.tsv',
    },
  },
}
```

## Slots
- `location: maybeFileLocation`: location of the `sequence_report.tsv` NCBI publishes with an assembly. It lists the RefSeq, GenBank and UCSC-style name of every sequence, so one file aliases them all without hand-writing a chromAlias table.
- `useNameOverride: boolean = true`: forces usage of the UCSC names over the NCBI style names from a FASTA

PileupBases.text:
# PileupBases (Display setting config, alignments plugin)

The pileup's `bases` layer, a cell per base over the reads: `show` turns it on and `encoding.color` names what it paints. It draws while `show` is on and a field is named. Its `filter` picks the modification calls the `modifications` and `bisulfite` fields paint, and `unmodified` adds the unmodified side in blue.

```js
{
  type: 'LinearAlignmentsDisplay',
  pileup: { bases: { show: true, encoding: { color: 'baseQuality' } } },
  encoding: { color: 'tags.HP' },
}
```
```js
{
  type: 'LinearAlignmentsDisplay',
  pileup: {
    bases: {
      show: true,
      encoding: { color: 'modifications' },
      filter: [
        { field: 'probability', gte: 0.5 },
        { field: 'code', oneOf: ['a'] },
      ],
    },
  },
}
```
```js
{
  type: 'LinearAlignmentsDisplay',
  pileup: {
    bases: {
      show: true,
      encoding: { color: 'modifications' },
      filter: [{ field: 'context', oneOf: ['CHG'] }],
      unmodified: { show: true },
    },
  },
}
```
```js
{
  type: 'LinearAlignmentsDisplay',
  pileup: {
    show: false,
    bases: { show: true, encoding: { color: 'modifications' } },
  },
}
```

## Slots
- `show: boolean = false`: draw the per-base layer over the reads, and its modification bars in the coverage band
- `encoding: PileupBasesEncoding sub-schema`: The layer's channels: `encoding.color`, the per-base variable.
- `encoding.color: AlignmentsBaseColor`: The per-base variable the layer paints.
- `filter: fieldPredicateList({ defaults: BASES_FILTER_DEFAULTS })`: The modification calls drawn, by `probability` (0 to 1), `context` (`CG`, `CHG` or `CHH`) and `code` (`m`, `h`, `a`, ...). Unwritten, a field keeps its default: `probability` at least 0.1, which only the by-type view applies, `context` CG,…
- `unmodified: PileupBasesUnmodified`: The unmodified side, in blue, under the `modifications` and `bisulfite` fields: the methylation view.

PileupBasesUnmodified.text:
# PileupBasesUnmodified (Display setting config, alignments plugin)

The pileup's `bases.unmodified` layer: the unmodified side of the `modifications` and `bisulfite` fields, in blue. Under `modifications` it paints every cytosine in the `context` a read's 5mC calls left implicit, the methylation view, and each other call more likely unmodified; the probability floor does not apply.

```js
{
  type: 'LinearAlignmentsDisplay',
  pileup: {
    bases: {
      show: true,
      encoding: { color: 'modifications' },
      unmodified: { show: true },
    },
  },
}
```

## Slots
- `show: boolean = false`: paint the unmodified side blue under the modifications and bisulfite fields

PileupMismatchesOpacity.text:
# PileupMismatchesOpacity (Display setting config, alignments plugin)

The pileup's `mismatches.encoding.opacity`: unset, every mismatched base draws opaque; `baseQuality` fades each one by its Phred base quality through a linear scale, by default from transparent at 0 to opaque from 50 up. A string is the field. A base whose read carries no qualities draws opaque.

```js
{
  type: 'LinearAlignmentsDisplay',
  pileup: { mismatches: { encoding: { opacity: 'baseQuality' } } },
}
```
```js
{
  type: 'LinearAlignmentsDisplay',
  pileup: {
    mismatches: {
      encoding: {
        opacity: {
          field: 'baseQuality',
          scale: { domainMin: 0, domainMax: 30, range: [0.2, 1] },
        },
      },
    },
  },
}
```

## Slots
- `field: maybeStringEnum (baseQuality)`: baseQuality fades each mismatched base by its Phred quality; unset draws them opaque
- `scale: PileupMismatchesOpacityScale sub-schema`: How a base quality maps to an opacity.
- `scale.domainMin: number = QUALITY_OPACITY_DOMAIN[0]`: the Phred quality that takes the first opacity in range
- `scale.domainMax: number = QUALITY_OPACITY_DOMAIN[1]`: the Phred quality that takes the last opacity in range
- `scale.range: stringArray = ['0', '1']`: the opacities, 0 to 1, at the two ends of the domain
- `scale.clamp: boolean = true`: a quality past the domain takes the nearer end of the range; off, the line runs on until the base is fully opaque or gone

RefNameAliasAdapter.text:
# RefNameAliasAdapter (Adapter config, config plugin)

Reads refName aliases for an assembly from a UCSC `chromAlias` file or any other tab-separated table of aliases.

Slots go inside a track's adapter: "adapter": { "type": "RefNameAliasAdapter", ... }.

Shorthand: uri, baseUri may replace the location slot.

Goes on an ASSEMBLY, under `refNameAliases` — not on a track. Writing
`refNameAliases: { uri: '...' }` is shorthand for exactly this adapter:
```js
{
  name: 'hg38',
  sequence: {
    type: 'ReferenceSequenceTrack',
    trackId: 'hg38-ReferenceSequenceTrack',
    adapter: {
      type: 'BgzipFastaAdapter',
      uri: 'https://example.com/hg38.fa.gz',
    },
  },
  refNameAliases: {
    adapter: {
      type: 'RefNameAliasAdapter',
      uri: 'https://example.com/hg38.chromAlias.txt',
    },
  },
}
```

## Slots
- `location: maybeFileLocation`: location of the alias table: a UCSC `chromAlias.txt`, or any tab-separated file whose rows each list the alternate names of one reference sequence (`chr1<TAB>1<TAB>NC_000001.11`). The table lets a `1`-named file load against a `chr1`-named…
- `refNameColumn: number = 0`: by default, the "ref names that match the fasta" are assumed to be in the first column (0), change this variable if needed — advanced
- `refNameColumnHeaderName: string = ''`: refNameColumnHeaderName — advanced

RibbonColor.text:
# RibbonColor (Display setting config, linear-comparative-view plugin)

The multi-way synteny display's `ribbons.encoding.color`: one color for every ribbon, or a field each ribbon carries: the record's strand (the default, while the channel names neither a field nor a color), a measurement through its preset (`identity`, `mapq`, `dnds`, `syntenicDepth`), or a column the table declares in `attributeColumns`, through the same slots as the synteny view's [SyntenyColor](../syntenycolor). A string is the field.

```js
{
  type: 'MultiWaySyntenyDisplay',
  ribbons: { encoding: { color: { value: 'grey' } } },
}
```
```js
{ type: 'MultiWaySyntenyDisplay', ribbons: { encoding: { color: 'strand' } } }
```
```js
{
  type: 'MultiWaySyntenyDisplay',
  ribbons: {
    encoding: {
      color: {
        field: 'strand',
        scale: {
          range: ['#1b9e77', '#d95f02'],
          names: ['Kept', 'Inverted'],
        },
      },
    },
  },
}
```
```js
{
  type: 'MultiWaySyntenyDisplay',
  ribbons: {
    encoding: {
      color: {
        field: 'group',
        scale: { domain: ['core', 'shell'], invalid: 'lightgrey' },
      },
    },
  },
}
```

## Slots
- `value: maybeColor`: The color of every ribbon while no field paints.
- `scale: ConfigurationSchema( 'RibbonColorScale', syntenyColorScaleSlots('RibbonColorScaleType'), colorScaleOptions('ribbons.encoding.color.scale'), )`: How the field's values map to colors, Vega-Lite's scale object.
- `legend: ConfigurationSchema( 'RibbonColorLegend', syntenyColorLegendSlots, colorLegendOptions, )`: The key this channel draws.
- `field: string = ''`: field to color each ribbon by: strand reads the relative strand between the two lanes the ribbon joins (the two placements' orientations multiplied out, not the drawn twist, so a flipped lane still shows its inversions); identity and dnds…

RowColor.text:
# RowColor (Display setting config, core)

The `rowColor` setting of the row displays: one ordinal color channel on the row axis. `field` names the row attribute whose values take the colors, `name` (the row itself) by default, and `scale.domain` and `scale.range` pair those values with CSS colors, so a color a reader sets on a row in the arrangement dialog is an entry under `name`. Each display paints it on the element that identifies a row: the quantitative display's plot, or the tint beside its label while a score gradient paints; the multi-row feature display's blocks; the multi-sample variant displays' label tint; the MAF…

```js
{
  type: 'LinearWiggleDisplay',
  rowColor: {
    scale: { domain: ['tumor', 'normal'], range: ['#b2182b', '#2166ac'] },
  },
}
```
```js
{ type: 'LinearMultiSampleVariantDisplay', rowColor: 'population' }
```
```js
{
  type: 'LinearMultiRowFeatureDisplay',
  rowColor: {
    scale: { domain: ['mom'], range: ['#b2182b'], unknown: '#cccccc' },
  },
}
```

## Slots
- `field: string = 'name'`: The row attribute whose values take the colors. Under `name` with no `scale`, stacked rows keep only a file's own colors, since the palette deals only where the rows share one panel; list the rows in `scale.domain` to color them.
- `scale: RowColorScale sub-schema`: Which color each value of the field takes.
- `scale.domain: stringArray = []`: the field's values that get a color, in order: under name, rows by name
- `scale.range: colorArray = []`: the CSS color each value in domain takes, in the same order
- `scale.unknown: maybeColor`: color for a value missing from domain. Unset: the next palette color where the display deals one, and the levels a preset field names, such as strand's, keep their own colors. A color: that color. "": no color dealt, so a feature paints…

SyntenyColor.text:
# SyntenyColor (View config, core)

The linear synteny, dotplot and circular views' `encoding.color`, which every track in the view paints with: one color for every alignment, or a field of each alignment — its strand, the sequence at either end, the anchor assembly's, the track it came from, a measurement through its preset (`identity` and `dnds` on a ramp, `mapq` in the alignments display's bins, `syntenicDepth` grey for a one-to-one ortholog and orange where genes share one), or a column the tracks declare in `attributeColumns`: a ramp for numbers and a color per label once a value is text, unless `scale.type` says otherwise…

```js
{ type: 'LinearSyntenyView', encoding: { color: 'strand' } }
```
```js
{ type: 'DotplotView', encoding: { color: { field: 'query' } } }
```
```js
{
  type: 'LinearSyntenyView',
  encoding: {
    color: { field: 'identity', scale: { scheme: 'magma', domainMin: 0.9 } },
  },
}
```
```js
{
  type: 'LinearSyntenyView',
  encoding: {
    color: {
      field: 'identity',
      scale: { type: 'threshold', domain: [0.95, 0.99] },
    },
  },
}
```
```js
{
  type: 'LinearSyntenyView',
  encoding: {
    color: {
      field: 'gene_group',
      scale: {
        domain: ['A1a', 'B1'],
        range: ['#1b9e77', '#d95f02'],
        names: ['Subgenome A', 'Subgenome B'],
      },
      legend: { title: 'Gene group' },
    },
  },
}
```

## Slots
- `value: maybeColor`: The color of every alignment while no field paints, in place of the view's default scheme: the match block of a synteny ribbon, whose insertions and deletions keep their colors, or a dotplot point. Unset, the default scheme paints.
- `scale: ConfigurationSchema( 'SyntenyColorScale', syntenyColorScaleSlots('SyntenyColorScaleType'), colorScaleOptions('encoding.color.scale'), )`: How the field's values map to colors, Vega-Lite's scale object.
- `legend: ConfigurationSchema( 'SyntenyColorLegend', syntenyColorLegendSlots, colorLegendOptions, )`: The key this channel draws.
- `field: string = ''`: field to color each alignment by: strand paints forward and reverse; query and target one color per sequence on that side, reference one per chromosome of the anchor assembly across a stack, track one per overlaid track (pinned under Track…

YChannel.text:
# YChannel (Display setting config, core)

A value axis written as Vega-Lite's `encoding.y`: its `scale`, the `axis` guide it draws, and `rules`, reference lines at chosen values across every band the scale rules. A rule naming no `color` draws in the one color the chrome rules every plot in. The wiggle display's also names the `field` it plots ([YFieldChannel](../yfieldchannel)).

A log depth axis floored at 1, with a labelled line at 30x:
```js
{
  type: 'LinearAlignmentsDisplay',
  coverage: {
    encoding: {
      y: {
        scale: { type: 'log', domainMin: 1 },
        rules: [{ value: 30, label: '30x' }],
      },
    },
  },
}
```

## Slots
- `rules.value: number = 0`: The rule's position on the axis, in the units the axis plots: a `-log10(p)` over a Manhattan plot, a depth over coverage.
- `rules.color: maybeColor`: The line's color, and its label's. Unset draws every rule in the one color the chrome rules plots in, so a threshold that means something particular — genome-wide significance, a diploid depth — is one an author paints, on the plot where…
- `rules.label: string = ''`: Free text drawn at the rule's right-hand end. JBrowse assigns it no meaning: "2 copies" over a coverage plot is a claim only the author can make, since no ploidy can be assumed.
- `scale: YScale`: `type`, `domainMin`, `domainMax`, `zero`, `fenceQuantile`, `constant` and `group`.
- `axis: YAxis`: `title` and `grid`.
- `rules: list of ValueScaleRule entries`: Horizontal reference lines, each `{ value, color, label }` or a bare number. An autoscaled end widens to keep every rule on the axis; a pinned end that excludes one drops it.
